philadelphia's district attorney is  getting national attention  in the fight against wrongful  convictions more wrongfully incarcerated  people were exonerated  of murder in philadelphia county last  year than anywhere else in america  since taking office in 2018 d.a larry  krasner  has exonerated 15 people jerika duncan  first spoke to krasner more than a year  ago  and checked in over the summer to hear  about reforming his office's culture  she's also caught up with one of those  15 exonerated people  terence lewis jerica good morning  good morning to understand terence's  terrence lewis's story rather you have  to go back  to 1999. he was 20 years old and  found guilty of second degree murder  sentenced to life in prison  well last year philadelphia prosecutors  dropped those charges  it's now been a year since terrace lewis  was released  at that moment that was that was sheer  genuine joy in may of 2019  after more than two decades behind bars  forty-year-old terence lewis walked out  of a pennsylvania prison  a free man coming from  the box of despair you know where i was  being warehoused  to be able to feel the breeze on that  side of the wall  and to get a genuine hug surreal  i was convicted of that reality was just  starting to set in when we first met  lewis last year  87 days after his release although i'm  grateful  is it's definitely been difficult  because i have been enslaved you know  mentally as well as physically it's been  over a year since you were released from  prison how do you feel  i would be remiss to say that you know  life has been a cakewalk initially it  felt like i was  leaving one prison to come home to  another prison  in 1999 lewis was convicted of  second-degree murder  and sentenced to life in prison  prosecutors built their case around the  testimony of one eyewitness  a drug addict i proved my innocence  unequivocally speaking  factually speaking it wasn't until 21  years later  and after the da's office expressed  quote really grave concerns about  whether lewis was guilty  that a judge threw out his conviction  and ordered lewis's freedom  i got the weights and i just zoned out  some out  think about freedom he sued the city of  philadelphia  that's because exonerees are not  automatically entitled to compensation  in pennsylvania  lewis alleged that police practices  including coercion suppression of  exculpatory evidence  and fabrication of evidence violated his  constitutional rights  this past june the city and lewis  reached a settlement  for 6.25 million dollars  i did 21 and a half years for a crime  that i did not commit  no money in the world can amount to what  i felt inside  he says he's using some of that money to  fight for justice  launching the terrence lewis liberation  foundation it's going to be our first  presentation  a non-profit partnering with u-pen  students to investigate claims  of wrongful convictions he has a sincere  belief  that there are innocent people sitting  in jail now which is surely true  lewis was inspired by philadelphia's  district attorney larry krasner  his conviction integrity unit helped  restore lewis's freedom  but krasner made it clear from day one  he wanted to take on  the entire system in his first week in  office in 2018  he fired 31 employees i did not enjoy it  but it was necessary to do especially as  i see these exonerations happen and  guess whose names  just keep coming up for having been  involved in convictions of innocent  people the people you let go  yep that story will be developing stay  tuned  i can tell you a lot of what has done  since i said stay tuned it's been in the  nature of education we put a ton  of time into educating the prosecutors  about things like the obligation to turn  over  information that helps the defense do  you feel you've done an effective job at  changing that  culture i feel we have although it's a  struggle  and we've had the opportunity to become  credible  within the system to people who may have  had misconceptions about what we were  trying to do  we are just as focused on going after  serious gun violence  as we are focused on making sure  innocent people do not sit in jail  out of a dozen shootings but gun  violence has spiked in philadelphia  since last year homicides have increased  about 40 percent  how do you convince people that you are  just as serious about  focusing on homicide at a time where  philadelphia is facing some of the worst  violent crime in over a decade let's be  fair about this  almost every big city in the united  states is in the same position  why do you think that is well i think  there's no question that it's related to  the pandemic for the first time  in probably close to 100 years we have  the philadelphia court system shut down  for months you have changes in policing  so that police  are making decisions not even to arrest  for certain offenses  when you have all of that going on you  have a perfect storm  in spite of that storm there is hope  says terence lewis  that no matter how long it takes justice  is possible i'm a believer from amongst  the believers in regards to  reform and eradicating the racial  disparities  and inequalities i'm very hopeful that  change in fact  is going to come the philadelphia police  department declined to comment on  lewis's case as you can hear and  tell that lewis is really focused right  now on his foundation  in helping other people like himself  tony  it's a powerful story thank you very  much we were talking about larry krasner  here at the table jericho  very very touched very moved very  thankful that he's taken the position  that he has  is he getting a lot of support  he is getting support from those who  support the ideas that he's talking  about and i think it's still  considered radical maybe not in  philadelphia but when you look across  the country  in terms of what he had to say even when  i asked him about  you know defunding the police and that  idea and him wanting to focus  more on not just putting people behind  bars  but really understanding why they're  there to begin with and looking at other  ways that we can use money  to fund schools to fund programs that  will keep people  from behind bars and listen the story of  terence lewis is a classic example  yeah and why this is so important i was  worried about him because he said he  goes from the box of despair  to almost going into another prison he  seems to be doing okay is he i mean  clearly  he has a you know money isn't going to  make the difference for what was taken  from him but i sure am glad he got some  money yeah
 

